      ":question/problem": "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})$, can be found by computing $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2x\\text{ }i+2y\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}) & =2(-\\frac{1}{2})\\text{ }i+2(\\frac{1}{2})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =-i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})}{‖∇f(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})‖} & =\\frac{-i+j}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-i+j}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i-j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
