      ":question/problem": "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}+x+y$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0).$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(0,0)$, can be found by computing $∇f(0,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x+y}+x+y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x+y}+x+y)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =({e}^{x+y}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{x+y}+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(0,0) & =({e}^{0+0}+1)\\text{ }i+({e}^{0+0}+1)\\text{ }j \\\\  & =2i+2j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(0,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(0,0)}{‖∇f(0,0)‖} & =\\frac{2i+2j}{\\sqrt{(2)^{2}+(2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2i+2j}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$"
